1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
UR5e six-axis robot arm — a complete control stack in MuJoCo simulation

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Six layers: kinematics, trajectory planning, control, abstraction, reinforcement learning environment, simulation

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Built from scratch, and verified by 20 unit tests

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Two learning approaches compared on the same reaching task: reinforcement learning, and vision-based behavior cloning

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Every number shown here is reproducible from the committed code and evaluation logs

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Layer 1 — Kinematics: forward and inverse kinematics with damped least squares, plus a numerical Jacobian

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Layer 2 — Trajectory: point-to-point and Cartesian straight lines, with quintic profiles and SLERP orientation

8
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Layer 3 — Control: position servo, torque PD with gravity compensation, and operational space control

9
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Layer 4 — Abstraction: an eleven-method interface contract; moving to real hardware needs one new class only

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Layers 5 and 6 — a Gymnasium RL environment on the official Menagerie UR5e model

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Experiment 1 — PPO reinforcement learning: one million steps, three random seeds

12
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Under a 2-centimetre threshold: 150 of 150 episodes succeeded, with 1.86 centimetres landing accuracy

13
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The reward is distance-based: every step that closes the gap is rewarded, and an exponential term sharpens the gradient near the target

14
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:18,000
One early fix mattered: sampling goals around the initial end-effector pose keeps them inside the two-second travel budget

15
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Before that fix, early training reached only 2 out of 10

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Experiment 2 — vision-based behavior cloning: 24 keyboard teleoperation demonstrations, 4,703 frames

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The policy takes a camera image plus a target coordinate, and outputs end-effector velocity; the target comes from an RGB-D detector

18
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:05,000
That detector was rebuilt after a controlled comparison: the truth-goal and detected-goal systems gave identical results in 43 of 50 episodes

19
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The original minimum-depth heuristic was hijacked by inconsistent edge pixels — 20.5 centimetres of error; centroid plus median depth brought it to about 5

20
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:30,000
At the 9-centimetre threshold, the cloned policy succeeds in 50 of 50 evaluations

21
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
At the 2-centimetre standard, it reaches only 0 to 10 percent across three training runs

22
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:52,000
That is not a tuning problem: my own demonstrations stop at 4.6 centimetres on average, and only about 10 percent finish inside 2

23
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:05,000
The policy reproduces its teacher's distribution — which is exactly what behavior cloning should do

24
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:15,000
I confirmed this by replaying my demonstrations through the physics engine, and by ablations on goal source and training data

25
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:30,000
A controlled experiment also refuted my own hypothesis: removing ten imperfect demonstrations made results worse — 0 out of 50, versus 5

26
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:42,000
So reinforcement learning beats the teacher, because it knows the dynamics and the reward presses directly on the threshold

27
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Imitation, by contrast, is bounded by the demonstrator — that is the finding, reported under both thresholds

28
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Code, evaluation logs and the full report: github.com/wang-robotics/ur5e-control-stack
